WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.910
Ah, wilderness survival.

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:05.920
There's nothing better to get your blood pounding
and your heart racing...even if you're only

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.049
experiencing dangerous situations vicariously
through TV shows like Survivorman.

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:15.831
First aired on the Discovery Channel in 2004,
the premise of Survivorman is simple: One

00:00:15.831 --> 00:00:19.900
man is dropped in the wilderness and left
to survive for seven days using nothing but

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.570
his wits and a sharp knife.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.259
But how realistic is it, really?

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:27.990
To get the real scoop, we talked to Les Stroud
from Survivorman, and he set the record straight

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:32.140
about reality TV — and what it actually
takes to survive.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.140
It's real

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.900
First things first: Survivorman is, by all
accounts, the real deal.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.530
Stroud comes from a background of teaching
wilderness survival.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:44.570
But, as he puts it, survivalists don't know
how to make a TV show, and television crews

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.399
don't know jack about survival.

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:48.430
"Nobody was doing it and the ones that did
it, they just sucked.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.910
It was just bad videos on stuff so I said
'you know what?

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.059
I'm a filmmaker and I'm a survival expert
why don't I put the two together."

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:58.280
Other survival shows have come after Survivorman,
but according to Stroud, they're all done

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.760
by what he describes as "TV people."

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:05.070
They care about doing things that look cool
on camera and get ratings, but they aren't

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.950
interested in teaching actual skills.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:08.950
Get lazy

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:12.890
Stroud stresses that every decision you make
in the wild needs to be weighed against its

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:13.930
energy cost.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:15.780
See a possible food source a mile away?

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.200
It better be enough food that it's worth the
hike.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.220
And how do you even know that's a mile away?

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.050
This leads to situations where that ridge
you may be looking at just hangs on the horizon

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:29.040
and never gets any closer, while you waste
your entire day chasing a wild goose.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.640
Or sheep.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.940
"baa."

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.960
Before you know it, it's dark and you don't
have any food or shelter.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.210
The one time to ignore that advice is when
you're being chased by a wild animal.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.020
Stroud has been chased out of the jungle by
a wild panther.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.640
And in situations like that, running suddenly
becomes acceptable: that's because maintaining

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.140
your energy levels won't matter if you don't
survive the next five minutes.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.110
Now that's just common sense.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:54.910
Man vs. Wild could get you killed

00:01:54.910 --> 00:02:00.470
Unsurprisingly, there's no lost love between
Les Stroud and Bear Grylls of Man vs. Wild.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.640
Stroud often calls out elements of Man vs.
Wild that he considers fake, like the numerous

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.170
allegations that Bear Grylls will build a
shelter on the show, but then takes a helicopter

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:14.340
to a hotel for the night, or that a raft he'd
built had been pre-assembled by the crew.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.909
"This raft needs to be strong, but it also
needs to be big enough to support me in the

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:19.909
sea swell."

00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:24.040
Stroud told us that trying to recreate some
of the stunts on Man vs. Wild could get you

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.730
seriously hurt or killed.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.990
In other cases, the survival tricks are just
useless, like trying to squeeze drinkable

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.670
water out of elephant dung.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.349
Les Stroud took to Reddit to clarify that
stunt, saying that, most likely, someone poured

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:39.499
water over the dung before Grylls drank it.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.020
Also?

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:40.520
Yuck.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.680
Survival myths are just as dangerous

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.969
Hollywood is notorious for showing us things
that look great on the big screen but work

00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:48.769
completely different in real life.

00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:52.879
If you ever thought that drinking the water
out of a cactus was a handy survival tip for

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:54.700
the desert, you couldn't be more wrong.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.739
The barrel cactus that you're thinking of
is actually filled with a vile liquid that

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:01.840
will make you puke, dehydrating you even more.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.480
And drinking your own urine reintroduces toxins
into your body.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.480
Also?

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:06.980
Yuck.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:07.480
Again.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.480
So what about starting a fire?

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.480
Stroud's favorite method:

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.700
"Wha - wha -what would you do differently?"

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:13.700
"Ah, just nothing.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.180
I just carry a big butane lighter."

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.129
That sounds a lot easier than rubbing sticks
for an hour.

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:22.819
Movies look cool, but no one should be watching
them for tips on how to survive a real worst

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:24.349
case scenario.

00:03:24.349 --> 00:03:25.349
Experiments

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:29.239
When Stroud hears about some new survival
trick, he doesn't try it out at home: he tries

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:31.890
it for the first time in the wild, and films
it.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:35.969
What this means is that sometimes, the viewers
witness him fail spectacularly.

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:38.730
But when one of these tricks works, the surprise
is real.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:42.379
He also throws himself curveballs to simulate
real situations.

00:03:42.379 --> 00:03:46.239
For example, to simulate a broken arm, he's
bound one of his hands.

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:49.790
No big shock there: It makes even the simplest
jobs a lot harder.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.329
He's also taken out a second person to see
if that made the survival situation easier

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:55.329
or worse.

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:56.329
The answer?

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:57.329
Both.

00:03:57.329 --> 00:04:00.920
Manual labor like building a shelter and other
tasks become far more manageable.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.499
But you also need to divide all your food
sources in half.

00:04:04.499 --> 00:04:08.549
It also helps with morale: just having someone
to talk to can go a long way.

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:13.209
Stroud has taken his son out on the show several
times, and describes it as a wonderful and

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:15.720
educational experience.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.200
It's...boring

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.870
It's easy to picture wilderness survival as
one big adventure, but according to Stroud,

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.400
the hardest thing about trying to survive
is the sheer boredom of it all.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:26.200
"That's the reality.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:28.840
Of survival."

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:35.640
Extreme boredom and loneliness, combined with
hunger and a lack of sleep, is a recipe for

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:36.650
depression.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:40.060
In real-life survival situations, people have
taken their own lives.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.560
The best thing you can do is get a fire going.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.720
Even if you don't need it for warmth or food,
a fire is the biggest morale booster you're

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.500
going to find in the wilderness.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.140
It provides a sense of accomplishment: you
did something.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.140
You're surviving.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.940
And that goes a long way.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.280
Thanks for watching!

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.090
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.370
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

